1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,620
For a long time, people believed that clever thinking

2
00:00:02,620 --> 00:00:03,520
belonged to humans

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:03,900
alone.

4
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:07,440
Then scientists started watching animals more carefully, and the

5
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:08,740
old idea began to fall

6
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:09,060
apart.

7
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:12,220
Today, we will meet four animals whose minds keep

8
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:13,820
surprising the people who study them,

9
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:16,840
a bird that makes tools, a sea creature with

10
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,600
no bones and an astonishing brain,

11
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:21,860
a giant that remembers friends for decades, and a

12
00:00:21,860 --> 00:00:23,400
pig that learns faster than you might

13
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:23,720
expect.

14
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:25,500
Let's begin with the

15
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:28,760
crow. In a famous experiment, a new Caledonian crow

16
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:29,100
named

17
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,600
Betty was given a short piece of straight wire

18
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,500
and a piece of food in a tube that

19
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:34,280
was too deep to

20
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:37,300
reach. Without any training, she bent the wire into

21
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:38,960
a hook with her beak and pulled the food

22
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:39,180
out.

23
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,140
Crows also drop nuts onto roads and wait for

24
00:00:42,140 --> 00:00:43,020
cars to crack them

25
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:44,560
open. In some cities, they have

26
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:46,400
learned to wait for the traffic light to turn

27
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:47,880
red before collecting the pieces,

28
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:49,760
and they seem to recognize human

29
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:52,720
faces. Researchers have found that crows can

30
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:55,100
remember a person who threatened them and can even

31
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:56,240
teach their friends to scold

32
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:56,760
that person

33
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,400
later. Next, the

34
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:01,720
octopus. An octopus has about 500 million nerve

35
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:02,060
cells,

36
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,380
roughly like a dog, and most of them are

37
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:05,800
in its arms rather than in its

38
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:06,020
head.

39
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:09,220
Each arm can taste, touch, and make small decisions

40
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:09,580
on its

41
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:11,140
own. It has three hearts and

42
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:13,300
blue blood, and it is a master escape

43
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,600
artist. Octopuses in aquariums have unscrewed jar lids

44
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:19,320
from the inside, slipped through gaps no wider than

45
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:20,760
a coin, and sneaked out of

46
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:22,720
their tanks at night to eat in a neighboring

47
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:23,820
tank before returning

48
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:24,140
home.

49
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:26,960
They also change color and texture in less than

50
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:28,960
a second to match a rock or a patch

51
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:29,480
of sand,

52
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:32,720
even though scientists believe octopuses cannot see color the

53
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:33,060
way we

54
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:34,340
do. How they pull that off

55
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:34,960
is still being

56
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:36,860
studied. Third, the

57
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,920
elephant. Elephants have the largest brain of any land

58
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:41,860
animal, and they use it for memory and for

59
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,720
emotion. An older female, called the matriarch,

60
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:45,360
leads each

61
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,180
herd. She remembers where the waterholes are, even those

62
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:49,020
not visited for

63
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:51,440
many years, and she remembers dangers from long

64
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:53,300
ago. Elephants greet one another with

65
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:56,180
touch and low rumbling sounds that travel for miles

66
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:56,540
through the

67
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:57,800
ground. They also seem to

68
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:58,220
mourn.

69
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:01,120
Herds have been seen standing quietly beside a fallen

70
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:02,840
member, gently touching the bones,

71
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:04,740
and returning to the place again and

72
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:06,700
again. They are one of the few animals that

73
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:07,220
pass the

74
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:09,840
mirror test, which means they appear to recognize themselves

75
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:10,440
in a

76
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:10,700
mirror.

77
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:11,840
And fourth, the

78
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:14,220
pig. Many people would not guess this

79
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:15,680
one. Pigs can learn their names,

80
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,240
can find their way through simple mazes, and in

81
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:19,720
some studies they learn to play a

82
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,800
video game by moving a joystick with their

83
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:24,540
snouts. They can also use a mirror to find

84
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:27,340
hidden food, which shows they understand what a reflection

85
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:30,440
is. Pigs are also social and playful,

86
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:31,940
and they recognize each other by

87
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:34,240
voice. If you grew up thinking of pigs as

88
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:34,540
simple,

89
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:35,580
the research says

90
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:37,660
otherwise. So we have four

91
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:39,580
minds. The crow is a toolmaker,

92
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:42,640
the octopus is an escape artist, the elephant is

93
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:43,160
a keeper of

94
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:43,480
memories.

95
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:44,920
The pig is a quick

96
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:46,780
learner. Let me line them up by what makes

97
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:47,280
each of them

98
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:47,580
special.

99
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:49,500
The crow's superpower is

100
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:51,680
invention. It looks at a problem and builds a

101
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:52,040
solution.

102
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:54,140
The octopus's superpower is

103
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:57,140
flexibility. Its body and its brain are spread across

104
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:57,480
eight

105
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:57,840
arms.

106
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:00,100
The elephant's superpower is

107
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:02,600
memory. It carries a whole community's history in its

108
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:02,820
head.

109
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:04,580
The pig's superpower is

110
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:06,960
learning. It picks up new rules quickly, and it

111
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:07,480
enjoys the

112
00:03:07,480 --> 00:03:07,720
game.

113
00:03:08,440 --> 00:03:09,220
Now, a thoughtful

114
00:03:09,220 --> 00:03:11,160
point. Scientists are careful

115
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:12,580
here. They do not say that animals

116
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:13,660
think exactly like

117
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:15,580
us. They say that intelligence comes in many

118
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:15,940
shapes.

119
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:18,240
A crow solves a puzzle in a way that

120
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:19,580
is different from the way an octopus

121
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:19,980
does.

122
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:22,680
An elephant remembers in a way no human can

123
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:25,420
match. Each mind evolved to solve the problems

124
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:27,540
of its own life, and each is impressive when

125
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:28,380
you meet it on its own

126
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:28,680
terms.

127
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,160
It also changes how we should treat these

128
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:33,420
animals. When you know that an octopus can

129
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:36,360
learn and an elephant can grieve, it becomes harder

130
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:37,320
to treat them as simple

131
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:37,640
things.

132
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:41,120
Many countries have introduced rules to protect animals that

133
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,060
show signs of feeling and understanding,

134
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:46,020
and scientists keep pushing those rules forward as new

135
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:46,360
evidence

136
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:46,780
arrives.

137
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:49,360
If you would like to see this yourself, you

138
00:03:49,360 --> 00:03:50,000
do not need a

139
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:50,440
laboratory.

140
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:52,480
Next time you pass a crow in a park,

141
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:53,620
stop for a minute and

142
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:53,920
watch.

143
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:56,600
See how it tilts its head, studies you, and

144
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:57,100
chooses its

145
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:57,420
moment.

146
00:03:57,780 --> 00:03:59,680
Visit an aquarium and look into the eye of

147
00:03:59,680 --> 00:03:59,780
an

148
00:03:59,780 --> 00:03:59,980
octopus.

149
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,500
Notice how it looks back, and how it seems

150
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:03,280
to be thinking about

151
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:03,500
you.

152
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:04,260
Let's

153
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:05,560
recap. Crows invent

154
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:08,720
tools. Octopuses solve puzzles with a brain spread across

155
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:09,060
eight

156
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:09,380
arms.

157
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:10,900
Elephants remember and

158
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,600
mourn. Pigs learn names and

159
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:12,920
games.

160
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,240
Four very different animals, and one big

161
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:15,620
lesson.

162
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:17,980
We have been sharing this planet with minds that

163
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:19,260
we are only just beginning to

164
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:19,700
understand.

165
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,640
And maybe that is the most exciting

166
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:21,980
part.

167
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:24,300
The next surprise might be sitting on a fence

168
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,100
right outside your

169
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:25,380
window.
